-- title:
--   Lemma 5.1, p.26 — $v$ of (44) is the value function under $P^*$ and solves the HJB equation (corrected: $X_\tau$, $v_t$ in (47), $u_2^{**}$)
-- statement:
--   Assume the standing assumptions, payoff symmetry, the indifference point $(\underline\pi,\overline\pi,\tilde z)$, the solution $(r^R,r^l)$ of (32), and $u_2<u_2^{**}$ (case (a.ii)). Let $v$ be the function (44) and $P^*$ a predictive prior with density generator (41), in a valid signal model. Then:
--
--   1. $v$ is the value function of the classical problem $\max_\tau E_{P^*}[X_\tau-c\tau]$ (42): for every $t\ge0$ and every stopping time $\tau\ge t$ with $E_{P^*}\tau<\infty$,
--   $$E_{P^*}[X_\tau-c(\tau-t)\mid\mathcal G_t]\le v(t,Z_t)\quad P^*\text{-a.s.},$$
--   with equality for the first time $\tau_t\ge t$ at which $Z\le\overline f(\cdot,r^l)$ or $Z\ge\underline f(\cdot,r^R)$, a stopping time with $E_{P^*}\tau_t<\infty$;
--   2. the HJB equation (45),
--   $$\max\{X(t,z)-v(t,z),\ -c+v_t(t,z)+\tfrac12\sigma^2v_{zz}(t,z)+f(t,z)v_z(t,z)\}=0,$$
--   holds at every $(t,z)$ with $z\ne\overline f(t,r^l)$, $z\ne\underline f(t,r^R)$, where $v$ is differentiable in $t$ and twice continuously differentiable in $z$; $f$ is the drift (46);
--   3. $v(t,\cdot)$ is continuously differentiable on $\mathbb R$;
--   4. (47): $-c+v_t+\tfrac12\sigma^2v_{zz}+fv_z=0$ for every $z\in(\overline f(t,r^l),\underline f(t,r^R))$.
--
--   This solves the single-prior problem under the conjectured worst case; the maxmin problem is reduced to it in `maxmin_value`.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: the augmented filtration $\{\mathcal G_t\}$ generated by $Z$ → any filtration $\mathbb G$ to which $Z$ is adapted and under which $Z/\sigma$ is a $(\mathbb G,P_0)$-Brownian motion; reason: the paper's filtration, with an augmentation that keeps its p.6 assertion true, is one of them, so the statement specializes to it; stopping times and density generators range over the larger class
--   - encoding: $P^\eta$ given by its density $dP^\eta/dP_0|_{\mathcal G_t}=\exp\{-\int_0^t\eta_s^2ds-\int_0^t\eta_sdW_s\}$ (printed without the factor $\tfrac12$) → the priors $Q$ with a density generator $\eta\in\Xi$, equivalent to $P_0$ on every $\mathbb G_t$, under which $W^\eta=Z/\sigma+\int_0^\cdot\eta_sds$ is a $(\mathbb G,Q)$-Brownian motion; reason: Girsanov characterization of $P^\eta$, since Mathlib has no Itô integral; for the paper's filtration it determines $Q$ on every $\mathcal G_t$, which is all the objective (9) depends on
--   - reading: §2: "each $\eta\in\Xi$ defines a probability measure ... $P^\eta$" (by Girsanov's theorem) → a standing property of the signal model: every bounded progressively measurable $\eta$ yields such a $Q$; reason: the paper asserts this as a consequence of Girsanov's theorem, which Mathlib lacks; it holds on the canonical space of continuous paths with Wiener measure and the natural filtration (the worst-case-prior milestone asserts that a valid model exists), and it fails on a filtration augmented by all null sets of $\mathbb G_\infty$, on which $P^\eta$ is singular to $P_0$
--   - encoding: $\eta_t(\omega)\in\Xi_t(\omega)$ for $dt\otimes dP_0$-a.e. $(t,\omega)$ → for every $(t,\omega)$; reason: clamping an a.e. selection to the interval $\Xi_t(\omega)$ gives a progressively measurable selection everywhere with the same $P^\eta$; the set of priors is unchanged
--   - encoding: posteriors $\underline m_t,\overline m_t$ by Bayesian updating (Liptser–Shiryaev, Theorem 9.1) → the closed form (12)–(13), as a function of $(t,z)$; reason: the paper's own formula; same objects
--   - generalization: a trajectory $(\tilde z_t)_{t\ge0}$ → a function $\tilde z:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$ with the defining identities for every real $t$; reason: the closed forms extend to all real $t$; the paper's trajectory is the restriction to $t\ge0$, and uniqueness on $\mathbb R$ implies uniqueness on $[0,\infty)$
--   - encoding: stopping times with values in $\mathbb R_+$ → stopping times in $[0,\infty]$ (`WithTop ℝ≥0`); on $\{\tau=\infty\}$ the payoff is evaluated at time $0$; reason: for $\tau\in\Gamma$ the set $\{\tau=\infty\}$ is null under every prior, so the objective is unchanged
--   - generalization: the worst-case prior $P^*$ with generator (41) → every predictive prior with density generator (41); reason: for the paper's filtration there is only one; the verification argument uses only the dynamics of $Z$ under the prior
--   - encoding: $v(t,z)=\max_{\tau\ge t}E_{P^*}[\cdots\mid Z_t=z]$ → Snell-envelope form: $E_{P^*}[X_\tau-c(\tau-t)\mid\mathbb G_t]\le v(t,Z_t)$ a.s. for every stopping time $\tau\ge t$ with $E_{P^*}\tau<\infty$, with equality for the first entry time into the stopping region; reason: Markov property of $Z$ under $P^*$; conditioning on $\mathbb G_t$ is the standard rigorous form
--   - correction: $X_{\tau-t}$ → $X_\tau$; reason: the payoff is the one at the stopping time, as in (42); $X$ depends on time through $\overline m_t,\underline m_t$ when $\theta_0+\theta_1\ne0$, so $X_{\tau-t}$ evaluates the payoff at the wrong date (at $\tau=t$ it would give $X(0,z)$ instead of $X(t,z)$, against (45))
--   - correction: (47): $-c+v(t,z)+\tfrac12\sigma^2v_{zz}+f v_z=0$ → $-c+v_t(t,z)+\tfrac12\sigma^2v_{zz}+fv_z=0$; reason: (47) is the continuation-region case of (45); as printed it fails in the Ellsberg instance, where $v_t=0$ and $-c+\tfrac12\sigma^2v_{zz}+fv_z=0$ in the continuation region while $v>1/2$
--   - encoding: the HJB equation (45) at every $(t,z)$ → (45) pointwise off the stopping-boundary curves, where $v$ is differentiable in $t$ and $C^2$ in $z$; $v(t,\cdot)$ is $C^1$ on $\mathbb R$; reason: $v$ is only $C^1$ across the stopping boundaries (smooth fit), where $v_{zz}$ does not exist; derivatives are `deriv`
--   - encoding: $f(t,z)$ of (46) → `drift`; reason: the paper uses the letter $f$ also for the boundary maps $\overline f,\underline f$ of p.21
--   - generalization: times $t\ge 0$ → every real $t$ for the deterministic functions of $(t,z)$; reason: the closed forms (12)–(13) are defined for every real $t$; restricting to $t\ge0$ gives the paper's statement
--   - generalization: case (a.ii): $r_1^l>\overline\pi$ → $u_2<u_2^{**}$; reason: by (37) the two are equivalent whenever (31) has a solution; $u_2<u_2^{**}$ also covers the case where (31) has none, and the paper's proof of (a.ii) uses only $u_2<u_2^{**}$
--   - correction: (34): $u_2^{**}=\tfrac{\hat c}{2}[\cdots]+\tfrac{u_{00}-u_{01}}{2}$ → $u_2^{**}=\tfrac{\hat c}{2}[\cdots]+\tfrac{u_{00}+u_{01}}{2}$; reason: the printed value contradicts (37) and the paper's identity $u_2^{**}=g_1(\overline\pi;C_1,C_2)$ (p.26): in the Ellsberg instance $\alpha=1/8$, $c=0.01$, $\sigma=1$, $\epsilon=0.04$ (all assumptions hold) the printed $u_2^{**}\approx0.126<u_2=1/2$ while $r_1^l=\hat r\approx0.5244>\overline\pi=0.52$; the corrected value $\approx0.5011$ equals $g_1(\overline\pi)$ and $v_0$ of (26)
-- source:
--   Epstein & Ji, Optimal Learning under Robustness and Time-Consistency, arXiv:1708.01890v2, Lemma 5.1, (42)–(47), pp.25–26

import Definitions.Def_EllsbergLearning_Model
import Definitions.Def_EllsbergLearning_ValueCandidates

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Set
open scoped NNReal ENNReal

namespace EllsbergLearning

/-- Lemma 5.1 (Epstein & Ji, arXiv:1708.01890v2, p.26), case (a.ii). Assume the standing
assumptions of §3.1 and payoff symmetry `u₀₁ = u₁₀`; let `(π̲, π̄, z̃)` be the indifference point,
`(rᴿ, rˡ)` the solution of (32), `u₂ < u₂**`, and let `v` be the function (44). Let `P*` be any
predictive prior with the density generator (41). Then:

1. `v` is the value function of the classical optimal stopping problem (42) under `P*`: for every
   `t ≥ 0` and every stopping time `τ ≥ t` with `E_{P*} τ < ∞`,
   `E_{P*}[X_τ − c(τ − t) | 𝔾_t] ≤ v(t, Z_t)` a.s., with equality for the first time `τ_t ≥ t`
   at which `Z ≤ f̄(·, rˡ)` or `Z ≥ f̲(·, rᴿ)`, which is a stopping time with `E_{P*} τ_t < ∞`;
2. (45): at every `(t, z)` off the curves `z = f̄(t, rˡ)` and `z = f̲(t, rᴿ)`, `v` is differentiable
   in `t`, twice continuously differentiable in `z`, and
   `max{X(t, z) − v(t, z), −c + v_t + ½σ²v_zz + f(t, z)v_z} = 0` with the drift `f` of (46);
3. `v(t, ·)` is continuously differentiable on all of `ℝ` (smooth fit at `f̄(t, rˡ)`, `f̲(t, rᴿ)`
   and smooth connection at `z̃_t`);
4. (47), corrected: `−c + v_t + ½σ²v_zz + f(t, z)v_z = 0` for every `z ∈ (f̄(t, rˡ), f̲(t, rᴿ))`.

**Formalization Note.** "`v(t, z) = max_{τ ≥ t} E_{P*}[X_{τ−t} − c(τ − t) | Z_t = z]`" is stated
in Snell-envelope form, conditioning on `𝔾_t`; the payoff is `X_τ` (printed `X_{τ−t}`), and (47)
has `v_t` (printed `v`). The hypothesis `u₂ < u₂**` is the reading of case (a.ii) (see
`theorem_4_2_a`). -/
theorem lemma_5_1 (p : Params) (hp : p.Valid) (hsym : p.PayoffSymmetry) (πL πU : ℝ)
    (zt : ℝ → ℝ) (hind : p.IsIndifference πL πU zt) (rR rl : ℝ)
    (h32 : p.Solves32 πL πU rR rl) (hu₂ : p.u₂ < p.u2StarStar πU rl)
    {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (M : SignalModel Ω) (hM : M.IsValid p.σ)
    (Q : Measure Ω) (hQ : M.HasGenerator p Q (M.etaStar p zt)) :
    let v := p.vA2 πL πU zt rR rl
    let gen : ℝ → ℝ → ℝ := fun t z => -p.c + deriv (fun s => v s z) t +
      p.σ ^ 2 / 2 * deriv (deriv (fun y => v t y)) z + p.drift zt t z * deriv (fun y => v t y) z
    let S : Set (ℝ × ℝ) := {q | q.2 ≤ p.fUp q.1 rl ∨ p.fLo q.1 rR ≤ q.2}
    (∀ (t : ℝ≥0) (τ : Ω → WithTop ℝ≥0), IsStoppingTime M.𝔾 τ → (∀ ω, (t : WithTop ℝ≥0) ≤ τ ω) →
        ∫⁻ ω, (τ ω : ℝ≥0∞) ∂Q < ⊤ →
        Q[fun ω => M.gain p τ ω + p.c * (t : ℝ) | M.𝔾 t] ≤ᵐ[Q] fun ω => v t (M.Z t ω)) ∧
    (∀ t : ℝ≥0,
        let τt := hittingAfter (fun (s : ℝ≥0) ω => ((s : ℝ), M.Z s ω)) S t
        IsStoppingTime M.𝔾 τt ∧ ∫⁻ ω, (τt ω : ℝ≥0∞) ∂Q < ⊤ ∧
          Q[fun ω => M.gain p τt ω + p.c * (t : ℝ) | M.𝔾 t] =ᵐ[Q] fun ω => v t (M.Z t ω)) ∧
    (∀ t z : ℝ, z ≠ p.fUp t rl → z ≠ p.fLo t rR →
        DifferentiableAt ℝ (fun s => v s z) t ∧ ContDiffAt ℝ 2 (fun y => v t y) z ∧
          max (p.X t z - v t z) (gen t z) = 0) ∧
    (∀ t : ℝ, ContDiff ℝ 1 (fun y => v t y)) ∧
    (∀ t z : ℝ, p.fUp t rl < z → z < p.fLo t rR → gen t z = 0) := by sorry

end EllsbergLearning
